    gas_commitment_bridge: { cell: "U", ev: "miso-130 DESCRIPTIVE pool census, NO prereg, cell stays U: model CC day-anchored night-off plants in July = 3 / 1,601 MW (2023), 0 / 0 MW (2024, 2025) — model CCs already run near-flat through summer nights (17.8–18.8 GW night vs 18.5–20.0 GW day), the same shape as PJM's kill; a future MISO charter must confront this census in its own PRE-REGISTERED pre-check (FINDING-miso130-c7-night-regime-2026-08-05.md §5). miso-133 adds the SLACK side of the same census, also DESCRIPTIVE (PREREG §2 S-4 declared it ungated, so the cell stays U and NO verdict is taken from it): at July night the keeper dispatches 6.1 % of assembled CT_PEAKER and 16.9 % of ST_GAS, leaving ~41 GW of idle non-coal gas capability against a 3.2 GW (2025) non-coal shortfall — CT_PEAKER availability would have to fall to ~20 % before its idle block alone stopped covering the whole shortfall. A bridge adds CAPABILITY, and MISO's overnight capability is ~10× the shortfall and idle, so the mechanism could not bind even if the pool were full (FINDING-miso133-overnight-identity-basis-2026-08-05.md §6) miso-246 (2026-09-09, ZERO LP, CELL STAYS U, NO VERDICT MINTED): the lane census measured the DIRECTION of the CC_REGULAR object this bridge would act on and it is ADVERSE. M-c1, on the keeper committed bench vs payload, shape-normalised, price deciles on the Indiana-hub RT: the model runs CC_REGULAR in EXCESS overnight (shape-deficit argmin at h0 in all three years; cheapest decile -96.6 / -63.1 / -800.8 MW) and SHORT at h19 (dearest decile +134.2 / +344.3 / +454.0 MW), span 270.8 -> 593.2 -> 1254.8 MW. A min-gen bridge ADDS overnight output, i.e. the side the model already over-runs, so it worsens the measured shape; miso-130s descriptive pool census agrees from the other end. NOT a candidate for the CC_REGULAR shape item; the cell stays U because no verdict is minted from a direction argument. FINDING-miso246-the-lever-queue-is-not-empty-and-neither-is-any-granted-ISOs-2026-09-09.md section 3c." },
