    import_shape_lever: { cell: "G", ev: "miso-262 (2026-09-19) -- CELL STAYS G; THE REFUSAL IS UNTOUCHED AND IS NOT RE-OPENED. ZERO LP. Two additions to the record, neither of them a new candidate. (1) miso-123's hour-of-day result EXTENDS TO 2020-2022 and to the aggregate grain: in 2020 the model's net imports peak at h13-h18 (7,628-7,745 MW) while the measured seam peaks at h01-h04 (6,611-6,724) and is flat thereafter; hourly r(model, measured) is 0.20/0.29/0.38/0.45/0.49/0.53 over 2020-2025, and the ORDERING-ONLY component of the schedule mismatch (schedule mismatch minus duration mismatch) is a near-constant 3.36/4.29/4.61/3.20/3.60/3.58 TWh in EVERY year, healthy ones included -- a standing property of a price-cleared ladder against a firm/JOA base, not a 2020 defect. (2) THE VOLUME HALF IS NOW MEASURED TOO and points the same way: the seam's annual volume error is the model's price bias times a constant, with a ~zero intercept (see seam_neighbour_hourly_ladder's miso-262 entry). So neither half of the seam's error is the seam's to repair. miso-123's stated re-open condition -- a SCHEDULING representation of the firm/JOA transfer base that can RAISE overnight flow, on an identification that is not the measured net interchange itself -- is unchanged and still UNMET; nothing in miso-262 licenses re-opening it. Record docs/FINDING-miso262-the-seam-is-a-price-transducer-2026-09-19.md §7. || miso-123 (FINDING-miso123-seam-hod-availability-closed-2026-08-04, PREREG-miso123, probe scripts/probes/_miso123_seam_hod_availability.py, transcript PROBE-miso123-seam-hod-availability-2026-08-04.txt); miso-114 §4 (the price-side alternative refuted ex ante)" },
