    capacity_market_supply_clearing: { cell: ".", fc: "U", ev: "UNTESTED, no transfer (rule 25 / 28d — capx D57): MISO's PRA is a sell-offer auction cleared against the RBDC, so a MISO instance of the clearing half is structurally admissible, but it would be its OWN lane with its own identification — the PRA's published offered / cleared ZRC by resource type as validation observables, a MISO net-ACR analogue (the PRA offer cap / RRP construction) and the seasonal RBDC grain (RC-1C) — never PJM's Manual 18 rules (design §4.9). The D31 accounting ratio already reconciles MISO's census to its OFFERED position; a clearing lane would have to state how the two compose (rule 19)." },
    capacity_going_forward_bar_published: { cell: ".", fc: "U", ev: "UNTESTED, no transfer (rule 25 / 28d -- capx D62, 2026-09-06): MISO runs the structurally closest analogue -- the Module E-1 / Attachment Y process, in which a Suspension or Retirement filing is tested against the unit's avoidable going-forward cost, and the PRA's sell-offer cap is the unit's own Reference Level. But MISO publishes NO generic technology-class default ACR table an owner may elect in its place, so there is no published operand to substitute for the ATB proxy, and the IMM's own avoidable-cost benchmarks are review inputs rather than an elective default. A MISO instance would have to identify its bar from MISO's own filings (and would land beside D53's sector gate and D51's dated-net ratio, which already partition and re-price the MISO screen), so it is a MISO lane's question on MISO's data. PJM's verdict fills nothing here (rule 25). No MISO partition exists in data/raw/capacity-market/avoidable-cost-rate/, so an armed gate is inert for every unit." },
